<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal Netherlands East Indies Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as KNIL, Royal Netherlands Indies Army</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> Army (KNIL) was the colonial army of the Dutch East Indies, formed in 1830 and separate from the army of the Netherlands. Its soldiers were mainly Javanese, Ambonese and Manadonese under European officers, and it fought the Padri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> wars that extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule across the archipelago. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1942, it fought the Indonesian Republic after 1945 and was dissolved in 1950.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army.jpg" alt="Royal Netherlands East Indies Army" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orange2000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Netherlands_East_Indies_Army_Coat_of_Arms_Tentara_Kerajaan_Hindia_Belanda_Lambang_Koninklijk_Nederlandsch-Indisch_Leger_Wapenschil_Logo_KNIL.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The KNIL was not part of the Royal Netherlands Army but a separate arm formed for service in the Netherlands East Indies. Its campaigns against indigenous groups included the prolonged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a> of 1873-1904, the crushing of resistance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in 1849, the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> and Karangasem in 1894 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> interventions in Bali of 1906 and 1908. After 1904 the colony was considered pacified, and the army turned mainly to military policing and to defence against invasion.</p><p>By December 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces in the colony numbered around 85,000, the regular troops about 1,000 officers and 34,000 men, 28,000 of them indigenous. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> campaign of 1941-42 most of the KNIL was quickly defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Most European soldiers were interned as prisoners of war, and 25% of the prisoners did not survive. Some personnel escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and regrouped, and small units saw action in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> campaigns of 1944-45.</p><p>After the war the KNIL took part in two large campaigns, in 1947 and 1948, to re-establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control, and it and its Ambonese auxiliaries have been accused of war crimes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> recognised Indonesian sovereignty on 27 December 1949. On 26 January 1950 elements of the KNIL took part in the failed coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> planned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-westerling">Raymond Westerling</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamid-ii-of-pontianak">Sultan Hamid II</a>, and the army was disbanded by 26 July 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>At first half the army was European and half indigenous, but from the late 1830s the ratio became one to three, as European volunteers were too few. In the 19th century it also recruited foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, among them Flemish, German, Swiss and French volunteers, and in limited numbers slaves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a>. In 1884 it counted 13,492 European, 14,982 Indonesian and at least 1,666 Eurasian recruits under a wholly European officer corps. The largest share of the native troops had always been Javanese and Sundanese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At its disbandment the KNIL numbered 65,000, of whom 26,000 joined the new Indonesian Army. Distrust between the mainly Ambonese KNIL troops and the Javanese-dominated republican army led to clashes at Makassar in April 1950 and the attempted secession of the Republic of South Maluku in July, both suppressed by November. About 12,500 Ambonese KNIL soldiers and their families chose temporary resettlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The traditions of the KNIL are kept by the Regiment Van Heutsz of the Royal Netherlands Army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Royal Netherlands East Indies Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Royal Netherlands Indies Army</b> (KNIL), formed in 1830, was a colonial force of mainly Javanese, Ambonese and Manadonese soldiers under European officers, supported by the navy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its wars were fought mainly in the colonies by the <b>Royal Netherlands East Indies Army</b> (KNIL), above all the long and costly Aceh War in northern Sumatra from 1873, which lasted into the early twentieth century, and campaigns that brought Bali and other islands under direct rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Military</a></li></ul></section>
